R.append((
"""### 5.4 A locked four-protein panel improves discrimination over MELD in external validation

The cohort was split by centre into a derivation set of 5 centres (n=995, 116 events) and a validation set of 8 centres (n=395, 46 events). LASSO-Cox applied to the 14-protein candidate pool retained five proteins at the concordance-optimal penalty (λ*=0.038): TNF-RI, Renin, DR6, SREC-II and Mesothelin (Fig 4A). Mesothelin was removed because it was selected in only 56.5% of 200 bootstrap resamples and lost significance once MELD was in the model (P=0.67); the resulting 4-protein panel performed identically in validation (AUC 0.935 vs 0.935; Supplementary Fig S1D), and all four retained proteins were selected in ≥79% of bootstrap resamples (SREC-II 92.5%, TNF-RI 91.0%, Renin 87.5%, DR6 79.0%; Supplementary Fig S1E).

In the derivation set, the panel added to MELD raised the C-index from 0.818 to 0.877. In the independent validation set, MELD achieved a C-index of 0.887 and an AUC at 90 days of 0.904, values that were not improved by MELD-Na (0.888 and 0.908) and were lower for a 4-organ CLIF-type organ-failure sum (0.865 and 0.881). The panel alone was inferior to MELD (C-index 0.864; AUC 0.898), whereas MELD plus the panel was superior to every comparator (C-index 0.906; AUC 0.935; Fig 4C, Table 2). The gain in AUC at 90 days was +0.031 (95%CI +0.004 to +0.063; bootstrap P=0.028); the gain in C-index was +0.019 (95%CI −0.008 to +0.051), which in a validation set of 46 events cannot be separated from zero. At 1 year the corresponding AUCs were 0.857 for MELD plus the panel and 0.814 for MELD.

Reclassification favoured the panel: the categorical-free NRI was +0.081 and the IDI +0.798. Observed 90-day event rates rose monotonically across quintiles of predicted risk—0%, 0%, 1.3%, 10.1% and 46.8%—so that the fifth of patients with the highest predicted risk accounted for the large majority of events (Fig 4D).

When the four proteins were entered simultaneously with sex and MELD in the derivation set, each retained an independent contribution: TNF-RI HR 1.38 (P=0.015), Renin 1.36 (P=0.006), SREC-II 0.76 (P=4.3×10⁻⁶) and DR6 1.57 (P=9.6×10⁻⁴). In the smaller validation set, TNF-RI (HR 1.68, P=0.016) and Renin (HR 1.73, P=0.004) remained independently associated, whereas SREC-II (0.90, P=0.58) and DR6 (1.21, P=0.42) did not; their contribution is captured at the level of the combined model rather than as stand-alone adjusted effects, and the wide confidence intervals in 46 events are compatible with the derivation-set estimates.""",
"""### 5.4 A locked five-protein panel improves discrimination over MELD in external validation

The cohort was split by centre into a derivation set of 5 centres (n=995, 116 events) and a validation set of 8 centres (n=395, 46 events). The derivation-only screen produced the 91-protein candidate pool; stability selection at the 1-SE penalty retained six proteins (TNF-RI 96.3%, DR6 96.0%, Renin 95.7%, ANG-2 95.7%, FGF-5 94.0%, Bcl-w 86.7%), the ≥80% assayability rule removed FGF-5 and Bcl-w, and backward elimination from the joint model removed ANG-2 (P=0.63), locking {TNF-RI, DR6, Renin}. The parallel ≥80%-quantified protocol locked {TNF-RI, DR6, Renin, SREC-II, GH}, and the locked panel was the union of the two: **TNF-RI, DR6, Renin, SREC-II and GH** (Fig 4A; Supplementary Fig S2A).

In the derivation set, the panel added to MELD raised the C-index from 0.818 to 0.879. In the independent validation set, MELD achieved a C-index of 0.887 and an AUC at 90 days of 0.904, values that were not improved by MELD-Na (0.888 and 0.908) and were lower for a 4-organ CLIF-type organ-failure sum (0.865 and 0.881). The panel alone was inferior to MELD (C-index 0.868; AUC 0.902), whereas MELD plus the panel was superior to every comparator (C-index 0.910; AUC 0.939; Fig 4B, Table 2). The gain in AUC at 90 days was +0.035 (95%CI +0.010 to +0.067; bootstrap P=0.002); the gain in C-index was +0.023 (95%CI not reported, 46 events), which in a validation set of 46 events cannot be separated from zero. At 1 year the corresponding AUCs were 0.855 for MELD plus the panel and 0.814 for MELD.

Reclassification favoured the panel: the categorical-free NRI versus MELD was +0.715 and the IDI +0.079. Observed 90-day event rates rose monotonically across quintiles of predicted risk—0%, 0%, 2.5%, 6.3% and 49.4%—so that the fifth of patients with the highest predicted risk accounted for the large majority of events (Fig 4D).

The incremental contribution of the two additional proteins was quantified against the pre-specified parsimonious alternative. Adding SREC-II and GH to MELD plus {TNF-RI, DR6, Renin} changed the validation AUC by +0.006 (95%CI −0.004 to +0.017; P=0.24) and added no reclassification (NRI −0.29, IDI −0.0003), but the two terms were jointly significant in the derivation set (likelihood-ratio test, LR 25.6 on 2 df, P=2.7×10⁻⁶; AIC 1000.6 → 979.0), and both improved the Brier score (0.0610 vs 0.0637) and the mean net benefit (0.0616 vs 0.0591). We therefore report the 5-protein panel as primary and the 3-protein model as a pre-specified parsimonious alternative (ΔAUC +0.028, 95%CI +0.004 to +0.061, P=0.023), while stating explicitly that the validation set cannot separate the two.

When the five proteins were entered simultaneously with sex and MELD in the derivation set, each retained an independent contribution: TNF-RI HR 1.34 (P=0.035), DR6 1.67 (P=1.9×10⁻⁴), Renin 1.33 (P=0.008), SREC-II 0.78 (P=9.3×10⁻⁵) and GH 0.75 (P=0.002). In the smaller validation set, refitting the same model gave TNF-RI (HR 1.54, P=0.037) and Renin (1.68, P=0.0066) as independently associated, with DR6 (1.30, P=0.28), SREC-II (0.89, P=0.52) and GH (0.70, P=0.097) directionally concordant but not separable in 46 events (Fig 4C); TNF-RI and Renin are therefore the components that survive every derivation and validation choice made here, and the contribution of the remaining three is captured at the level of the combined model."""))

